\tag{fdip}
\title{Character expansion on a finite surface}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{The [two-element torus law](fdip-006R) counts plaquette signs.
For a noncommutative finite gauge group, irreducible characters replace
those signs. Averaging each edge pairs two representation matrices;
the remaining index sums count vertices. On a closed oriented surface,
Euler characteristic then determines the power of each representation's
dimension.}

\p{The face weight and the measure are explicit throughout. The
quaternion group and the binary tetrahedral group give finite examples
with the same two-dimensional spinor Wilson action. Their exact surface
partition functions and one-face loop expectations depend on their
different character tables. These are finite pure-gauge models; no
continuum limit or matter field is assumed.}

